WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.709
After its notorious run in the 90s, Beavis
and Butt-Head returned to MTV in 2011, but

00:00:05.709 --> 00:00:07.919
that ended up being a pretty big failure.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.160
So what exactly happened?

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.250
Keep watching to find out why the Beavis and
Butt-Head revival failed.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.560
In the 90s, Beavis and Butt-Head was more
than a little controversial.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.940
It upset a lot of people thanks to its crude,
rude, destructive, and antisocial characters.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.220
Beavis' love of playing with fire and its
supposed role in a real-life copycat act in

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:30.080
which a kid burned down his family home led
MTV to ban all references to fire in the series.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.980
Even a legislator criticized the show on the
floor of the United States Senate.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.780
That all just made Beavis and Butt-Head seem
super edgy.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.771
If not for the series testing the public's
appetite for sick and twisted animation, there

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.400
might not have been shows like South Park
and Family Guy following in its wake.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.060
“She said, 'Wood.'

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.800
That was cool."

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:49.520
"Yeah.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:50.020
Wood!

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:50.520
Wood!"

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:51.720
"You're right!

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:52.800
Wood!"

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.700
Beavis and Butt-Head made room for a vast
TV landscape full of adult animation, so much

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.810
so that by the time it reappeared on MTV in
2011, it didn't feel that provocative anymore.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.280
If anything, it felt like a cynical cash grab
to some viewers.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.110
USA Today interviewed students at the University
of North Carolina at Asheville about the revival,

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:15.660
and many of them felt the show was just playing
into rampant '90s nostalgia.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:16.780
As one one student put it,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.720
"I think it's a poor way for [creator] Mike
Judge to try and capitalize on the memories

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.240
of kids who grew up in the 90s."

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.680
Not only was the whole vibe of Beavis and
Butt-Head possibly played out by 2011, but

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:28.820
so was the show's content.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:33.340
It had struck a chord in the early 90s because,
despite being a crudely animated series, it

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.660
felt like a slice of real life.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.760
The title characters were a couple of bored
teens who watch TV, hate school, and busy

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:39.960
themselves with troublemaking.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:44.200
They seemed far more realistic than any other
portrayal of young adults on TV, like the

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.040
ones on Beverly Hills, 90210 or Saved By the
Bell.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:51.280
But when Beavis and Butt-Head returned in
2011, their activities still felt like a slice

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.940
of '90s life, instead of 2010s life.

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:58.020
Try as they might, the new Beavis and Butt-Head
writers had a tough time updating the characters

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.020
for the modern world.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:03.780
After all, they still wore the same T-shirts
bearing the names of AC/DC and Metallica,

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.630
two bands a little past their prime and no
longer favored by the average 15-year-old.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.920
While the Beavis and Butt-Head revival felt
like a 90s time capsule, its creative staff

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.680
and the powers-that-be at MTV did try to update
a big chunk of the show's format.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:22.080
When the series premiered in 1993, MTV aired
hours of music videos each day, and Beavis

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:25.450
and Butt-Head would scan through such outlets
to find videos they could either headbang

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:26.760
to or savagely mock.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:32.120
But by 2011, MTV had moved away from videos
and towards reality shows about teens and

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:33.120
young adults.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.700
And so, when Beavis and Butt-Head reemerged
that year, they would watch one or two music

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.780
videos per episode for old time's sake.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:43.840
But they would mainly provide catty commentary
on MTV's library of reality shows, such as

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.880
Jersey Shore or 16 and Pregnant.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:50.440
Unfortunately, this didn't really work out
as well as making fun of videos, and as a

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:54.349
result, Beavis and Butt-Head were strangers
in a strange land, lost souls from another

00:02:54.349 --> 00:02:54.880
time.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.080
Television is a business, and networks determine
what shows to renew and keep investing in

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.700
based on how many viewers they pull in, which
helps set advertising rates.

00:03:03.989 --> 00:03:07.739
Generally speaking, the better a show's performance
in the Nielsen ratings, the more likely it

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.800
is to be profitable and earn another season.

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:13.180
With this in mind, the end of the Beavis and
Butt-Head revival may have come down to a

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.290
simple business decision about viewership

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:18.270
Nostalgia-driven viewers initially turned
out for the revival.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:23.940
The premiere episode, broadcast on October
27, 2011, attracted 3.3 million viewers.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.060
In the coveted 18-49 demographic, it pulled
a 1.8 rating, defeating all other shows on

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.380
cable primetime that night.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.320
Still, Beavis and Butt-Head didn't bring in
as nearly as large of an audience as the show

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.220
it temporarily replaced in its time slot,
Jersey Shore.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:40.300
Still, MTV had to have been happy with those
initial numbers.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.580
But they definitely weren't pleased with the
ratings that subsequent episodes earned.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:48.480
Viewers didn't stick around from week to week,
with the season finale on December 29, 2011

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.560
drawing a paltry 919,000 total viewers.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.600
One major factor in MTV's decision to not
bring back the revived Beavis and Butt-Head

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.629
for a second season involved some good old
corporate interference, or rather, a lack

00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:01.120
thereof.

00:04:01.629 --> 00:04:06.040
While Mike Judge ended the series of his own
accord back in 1997 due to dwindling creative

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.060
energy, he felt recharged by the time the
reboot rolled around.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:13.019
And he apparently had plenty of gas left in
the tank because he didn't actually want the

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:15.300
show to end when it did for the second time.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.430
Sadly, the show never came back, in 2012 or
any year since.

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:21.600
As Judge told Mandatory in 2014,

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.760
"That kind of caught up in some kind of changeover
of the top brass of MTV."

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:29.900
Oftentimes, when new TV executives come in,
they want to focus on their own projects,

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.800
and not those of their predecessors.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:35.280
And that very well could've been what doomed
the revamped Beavis and Butt-Head.

